.TH WEAVE 1 "September 7, 2026" "weave 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
weave \- print ready tasks from a finite graph and observed outcomes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B weave
.RB [ -- ]
.I tasks.jsonl
< observations.jsonl > ready.jsonl
.br
.B weave help
.br
.B weave version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Weave validates a finite task graph and a current observation snapshot,
then prints unobserved tasks whose dependencies are all accepted.
It neither executes tasks nor verifies the truth of evidence references.
All input is validated before output. Empty output does not mean completion.
.SH RECORDS
Tasks require id, needs (an array of distinct existing task ids), and input
(any JSON value). Extra fields survive. Output preserves the original line
bytes and input order, with a newline terminator.
.PP
Input records end with LF or CRLF; an unterminated final record is accepted.
Only the line terminator is excluded from the task digest. A CR without a
following LF is payload. Output uses LF, or CRLF when the payload ends in CR,
so reading the output again preserves the same payload and digest.
.PP
Observations require id, task_sha256, and state. The digest is sha256:
followed by lowercase hex over the exact task line excluding LF or CRLF.
States are queued, running, accepted, rejected, and unknown. Terminal states
require evidence references with nonempty kind and ref strings.
Absence means not admitted. Duplicate observations, stale hashes, missing
dependencies, cycles, duplicate JSON keys and inconsistent states fail.
Known field names are case-sensitive. Invalid UTF-8 and unpaired escaped
Unicode surrogates fail, including in extension fields and evidence references.
.SH OPTIONS
.B help, -h, --help
print usage without reading either input.
.PP
.B version, --version
print the version without reading either input.
.PP
.B --
treats the following task filename literally, including help, version, or -.
Observations always come from standard input; - does not redirect task input.
.SH LIMITS
Each input: 16 MiB. Each line: 1 MiB. Each stream: 10000 records.
Graph: 100000 edges. JSON nesting: 64 levels. IDs: 256 UTF-8 bytes.
Line limits exclude LF or CRLF; stream limits include them. The outermost
object is nesting level one, and empty containers count toward the limit.
IDs must be nonempty and their size is measured after JSON string decoding.
Blank lines are invalid.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 means a valid projection, possibly empty.
1 means invalid or inconsistent data.
2 means bad usage or an operating failure, including output failure.
Output failures may leave a partial stream; check the exit status before
admitting work. Normal operating-system signal handling applies, including
a possible SIGPIPE termination when a downstream reader closes the pipe.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
No Weave-specific environment variables are read by the filter.
.SH SEE ALSO
ask(1), ply(1), trail(1)
